    "q-104587": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104587",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=12({t}^{2}-t)$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{g(t)\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-24{t}^{2}+12t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-8{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-8+6)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
