    "q-105461": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105461",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product.",
      "worked_solution": "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
